\section*{Abstract}
Physics-anchored cryptographic systems offer a fundamentally different security foundation compared to algorithmic randomness. Here we introduce a multi-physics simulator that couples vascular tissue dynamics, biorheological fluid flow, and astrophysical density fields within a unified Hamiltonian framework. Biological state vectors $(q_{bio}, p_{bio})$, astrophysical flux fields derived from FITS cubes, and interaction terms are combined into a composite Hamiltonian whose chaotic regimes serve as a non-deterministic entropy source for cryptographic key generation. Chaotic divergence is quantified via Lyapunov exponents and mapped into key material through the Blackwell Encryption Layer (BEL). This architecture provides a reproducible, physics-anchored cryptographic pipeline with provenance and Hamiltonian integrity. We detail the mathematical formulation, system architecture, numerical methods, and validation pathway.

\section*{Introduction}
Cryptographic systems typically rely on pseudo-random number generators, which are vulnerable to advances in computational modeling and hardware acceleration. A physics-anchored alternative, in which entropy is derived from chaotic divergence in multi-domain Hamiltonian systems, provides a fundamentally different security paradigm.

The H-WAVE-CIE simulator integrates three physical regimes: vascular tissue networks, biorheological fluid flow, and astrophysical density fields. These domains are unified through a composite Hamiltonian,
\[
H = H_{bio}(q_{bio}, p_{bio}) + H_{astro}(q_{astro}, p_{astro}) + H_{interaction}(q_{bio}, q_{astro}),
\]
which governs the evolution of the full state vector
\[
x = (q_{bio}, p_{bio}, q_{astro}, p_{astro}).
\]

Chaotic regimes emerge when the system is driven through parameter sweeps and boundary conditions that induce sensitive dependence on initial conditions. Divergence is quantified using Lyapunov exponents, and chaotic attractor trajectories are sampled to generate cryptographic keys.

\section*{Results}

\subsection*{Coupled Multi-Physics Dynamics}
The biological subsystem includes vascular tissue networks, MASA-based biorheological fluid solvers, voxel-level atomic state modeling, and heat-flux transport. The astrophysical subsystem incorporates cosmic density fields and FITS cube flux data. Coupling these domains produces high-dimensional Hamiltonian dynamics with tunable chaotic regimes.

\subsection*{Chaotic Regime Formation}
Chaotic behavior is quantified using Lyapunov divergence:
\[
\lambda(t) = \frac{1}{t}\ln\frac{\|\delta x(t)\|}{\|\delta x(0)\|}.
\]
Positive $\lambda$ values indicate chaos. Divergence profiles exhibit high entropy density and sensitivity to initial conditions.

\subsection*{Cryptographic Key Generation}
Chaotic attractor trajectories are sampled at discrete intervals $\Delta t$ and mapped into key material:
\[
K(t_i) = g(x(t_i), \lambda(t_i)).
\]
The BEL module applies XOR-based encryption using $K(t)$, producing physics-anchored encrypted streams.

\section*{Discussion}
The H-WAVE-CIE architecture introduces a new class of cryptographic systems grounded in multi-physics Hamiltonian dynamics. Unlike algorithmic randomness, physics-anchored entropy cannot be reproduced without reconstructing the underlying physical regime. This provides inherent provenance, reproducibility, and resistance to brute-force simulation.

\section*{Methods}

\subsection*{Hamiltonian Formulation}
The composite Hamiltonian is defined as above. Gradients are computed as:
\[
\nabla H = \left(
\frac{\partial H}{\partial q_{bio}},
\frac{\partial H}{\partial p_{bio}},
\frac{\partial H}{\partial q_{astro}},
\frac{\partial H}{\partial p_{astro}}
\right).
\]

\subsection*{Numerical Integration}
A symplectic leapfrog integrator preserves Hamiltonian structure. Voxel-level solvers handle multi-scale interactions. FITS cube readers process astrophysical flux fields.

\subsection*{Chaos Quantification}
Lyapunov exponents are computed using standard divergence metrics. Chaotic attractor trajectories are sampled at discrete time intervals $\Delta t$.

\subsection*{Key Generation}
Key material is derived using:
\[
K(t_i) = g(x(t_i), \lambda(t_i)).
\]

